welcome back to cbs this morning we  just showed you how many parents are  worried about the new school year  as we continue our special school  matters series we're joined by cbs news  senior medical correspondent dr tara  narula  and she's here to answer your questions  tara good morning  we heard uh we heard some of the  questions before we're going to go to  them now  the first comes from melanie who wants  to know what to do if her child has  chronic  seasonal allergies she asks are they  going to be sent home  all the time that's such a great  question and i know a lot of parents who  are wondering the same thing if my child  goes to school and sneezes or coughs  or says they don't feel well what  cascade is that going to trigger  and so this is really a great time to do  two things plan  and communicate so you want to  communicate and plan with your school  and your school nurse if they have one  and really tell them you know my child  has seasonal allergies this is typically  how they present this is how we treat  them  and then you want to talk to your  pediatrician or allergist and get them  on a good regimen a lot of  allergy planning and treatment involves  starting medications early  and then one last tip is that the kids  are going to be wearing masks those  masks can attract pollen  and hold it in so you want to make sure  you're washing those regularly and one  little plug for vaccination very  important during flu season  because that's something else that can  potentially be confused with coven  good advice there the next question  comes from lindsay who  asked how will the lack of in-person  social interaction  between our younger kids impact their  learning and development  i love this question because i love the  fact that she's talking about social  emotional learning  you know this is really critical now  almost more than ever  and when we talk about social emotional  learning we're talking about kids  learning things like  empathy how to make decisions how to  communicate  how to regulate their emotions and even  talk about their feelings  and really that's something that needs  to be built into the classroom and while  it's harder to do  with distance learning it's something  that can be done online teachers can  incorporate that into their curriculum  families can talk about those things at  home and really  ask their children how they're feeling  and teach them how to regulate their  emotions  and especially if they have siblings  it's something they can work on and i  think  the important thing here is that kids  are resilient and if anything this is  going to build resilience in our  children and really  ultimately what they need is a loving  safe secure environment so as long as  you're providing that  our kids are going to be just fine sarah  with so many students learning remotely  one  one viewer wants to know if there are  concerns about young children  spending so many consecutive hours in  front of a screen  well i have two little ones as well and  we talk a lot about screen time in our  house and  it's not really a black and white issue  right now we do need to be flexible and  in fact the american academy of  pediatrics  released a statement in march  acknowledging the fact that we are going  to see increase in screen time use  the two important things really being  the content and the context and so  using those screens to interact with  family and friends is important  obviously learning to using it to learn  is going to be critically important  and what the aap really suggests is that  you preserve offline activities so  things like  the fact that it doesn't interrupt with  sleep or reading or  even connecting with family exercise and  just down  time to reflect we all need a little of  that we also heard from sarah who is a  grandmother  who will be caring for her 8 and 12 year  old grandchildren after school  she asks how do i protect myself and my  husband  from possible spread of covet while  they're here  well this is a tough question and the  reality is we're learning more and more  each day about how much  children really do transmit even if  they're asymptomatic  and i think you know clearly there are  things you can do in the home like  cleaning disinfecting  having proper ventilation opening the  windows but then when the kids come home  there's little steps you can take like  having them take their shoes off at the  door making sure they wash their hands  leaving their backpacks and belongings  maybe by the front door  and even changing their clothes all of  these little things can go a long way  but it's definitely  you know a tricky situation when you  have people who are elderly caring for  young children and those  elderly may be at increased risk of  severe complications  one more quick one tariff we could pam  wants to know how long covid could  actually live on books and surfaces  well we still think that the primary  route of transmission is droplets or  even potentially airborne and not really  through contact  there have been some studies that have  shown that kovit can live on surfaces  for hours to days  so cleaning and disinfection is going to  be important and really the cdc is  recommending that schools not only do  that but really avoid having kids  share things like pencils or markers or  things that couldn't be cleaned  very easily to have their own cubby and  locker so  taking these little steps is going to be  important because we don't have all the  answers yet on exactly how long  it can live on services that's part of  what makes it so confusing tara thank  you so much  we would also like to hear from students  we want to hear your questions  about the school year whether your  classes are in person or online  email your questions to coronavirus at  cbsnews.com
 

